netpbm manual pages -- the documentation these programs were shipped with.WHERE THESE CAME FROM.  The 169 programs in /dd/CMDS/NETPBM identifythemselves as "Netpbm 1 March 1994" and were built on 19 August 1995.  Theirsource release, netpbm-1mar1994.tar.gz, was recovered from an archived copyof ftp.x.org/R5contrib -- the original FTP site is long gone.  These are thatrelease's own man pages, unedited apart from line endings.168 of the 169 programs have a page.  Only pnmhisteq does not: it was added amonth after our binaries were built, in an unofficial patch release.READING ONE.  nroff is on this disk and SYS/login points TMACDIR at themacros it needs:    nroff -man /dd/DOC/netpbm/pnmscale.1THE -man MACROS WERE INCOMPLETE, AND WERE EXTENDED.  Rosenkranz's nroffships what its own README calls a "crude man package", defining .TH .SH .SS.PP and .I and nothing more.  Every man page also uses .B, .TP, .IP, .RS,.BR, .RI and .IX, and without those a page comes out buried in"unrecognized command" lines.  LIB/tmac.an now defines them too.  Theoriginal is kept beside it as LIB/orig.tmac.an.What is still imperfect, measured across all 172 pages: font alternation isapproximate, because this nroff has no diversions, so .BR and its relativesjoin their arguments rather than interleaving fonts exactly.  Fourtypesetter-only requests (.cs .ss .nh .hy) are ignored, which affectsnothing you can see on a terminal.  Two pages carry an upstream typo,.OPTIONS where .SH OPTIONS was meant.  A long SYNOPSIS line occasionallydrops a fragment.  Everything else formats correctly.FOUR OF THESE ARE NOT PROGRAMS.  pbm.5, pgm.5, ppm.5 and pnm.5 describe theFILE FORMATS rather than any command.  Start with pbm.5 if you want to knowwhat these programs are passing to each other.WHAT THEY DO AND DO NOT TELL YOU.  They are reference, not introduction.They give every option a program takes.  They will not tell you why youwould want it: pbmtoatk.1 says, in full, that it reads a portable bitmap andproduces an Andrew Toolkit raster object -- true, and no help whatever ifyou have never heard of the Andrew Toolkit.  DOC/README-NETPBM is ourattempt at the other half.COPYING.  COPYRIGHT here is the package's own notice.  Most pages carry JefPoskanzer's grant of permission to use, copy, modify and distribute withoutfee; the package as a whole asks that redistribution be non-commercial.  Thesame terms already covered the binaries.  See SOURCES.txt.